{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Procedural and Object Oriented Programming", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 2, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear/use to talk about procedural and object oriented programming.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Talk about principles, concepts, characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.   Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob02re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "The Future With Will, Going to, the Present Continuous and the Present Simple", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Let's review the following structures. Remember they are used in many different ways: You can use</span> <strong>simple present</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>and</span> <strong>present continuous</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>to talk about activities that take place in the present in general or that are in progress in the present. Nevertheless,</span> <strong>Simple present,</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>along with</span> <strong>Going to, present continuous</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>and</span> <strong>Will </strong>, <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>can also be used to talk about future actions. Let's see how they work!", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<strong>Simple Present:</strong><br/> We use the <strong>simple present</strong> to talk about permanent habitual facts in the present, but it can also be used as part of a prospective time from the present; this means to refer to future actions. Let's remember the structure of the <strong>simple present</strong>:<br/><br/>   subject  + verb in simple present  + complement<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">Programming processes <strong>finish</strong> next week.<br/>  My co-workers <strong>have</strong> to design several software apps for next month.</span><br/><br/>  Don't forget to add the <span class=\"italic\"><strong>-s</strong></span> or <span class=\"italic\"><strong>-es</strong></span>  to the verb in the third person affirmative form.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">The system <strong>starts</strong> working tomorrow at 08:00 a.m.</span><br/><br/>  For negative and interrogative forms in <strong>simple present</strong>, use the modal verb <strong>do</strong> which in the third person becomes <strong>does.</strong> The main verbs of the sentence keep their base form; we do not add the <span class=\"italic\"><strong>-s</strong></span> when we formulate negative or interrogative statements.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\"><strong>Do</strong> we <strong>have</strong> the programming meeting tomorrow?<br/> She <strong>doesn't</strong> finish writing new codes on time.</span><br/><br/>    <strong>Present Continuous:</strong><br/><br/>  We use the <strong>present continuous</strong> to talk about actions that are happening at the moment of speaking, or/and temporary situations; it is also used to talk about actions in the future.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">I'<strong>m working</strong> on my apps <strong>over the weekend</strong>.<br/>  <strong>Next year,</strong> we'<strong>re developing</strong> an amazing software system.</span><br/><br/>  The structure of the <strong>present continuous</strong> has the verb <strong>be</strong> and an action verb with -<strong>ing</strong> at the end. To make the negative statement, we just have to add the particle <strong>not</strong> next to the verb \u201c<strong>be</strong>\u201d. Don't forget to conjugate the verb <strong>be</strong>.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">We'<strong>re working</strong> on the new software next month.<br/> He'<strong>s not working</strong> on that project until next year.</span><br/><br/>  To make questions in the <strong>present continuous</strong>, we place the verb \u201c<strong>be</strong>\u201d (conjugated) before the subject. Just as in the simple present, only a <strong>w-h</strong> word can come before the verb <strong>be</strong>.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\"><strong>Are</strong> we working on the project next week?<br/> <strong>What software</strong> are we working on tomorrow morning? </span><br/><br/>   <strong>Future with Going To:</strong><br/> We use <strong>be going to</strong> to talk about plans in the future.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">I'<strong>m going to</strong> design a software next week.<br/> We <strong>are going to</strong> visit the client to collect requirements next month. </span><br/><br/>  To make the negative and interrogative forms, we add <strong>not</strong> to the verb <strong>be</strong> conjugated. For questions, we put the verb <strong>be</strong> before the subject, just as we do in the present continuous.<br/><br/>   Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">We <strong>are not</strong> going to continue with that project.<br/> Why <strong>are you</strong> going to quit the programming team?</span><br/><br/>  <strong>Future with Will:</strong><br/> We use the modal verb <strong>will</strong> to talk about plans in the future.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">I <strong>will design</strong> an accounting software for IBC.<br/> We <strong>will visit</strong> the client to collect requirements in the next months.</span><br/><br/>  The structure of sentences with <strong>will</strong> is quite similar to the simple present, with the difference that instead of using the auxiliary verb <strong>do</strong> or <strong>does</strong> we use <strong>will</strong>. To make the negative and interrogative forms, we add <strong>not</strong> after <strong>will</strong>. For questions, we place <strong>will</strong> before the subject, just as we do in the simple present.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">We <strong>will not</strong> (<strong>won't</strong>) continue with that project next year.<br/>  <strong>Will</strong> you work on that project for the next coming months?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "As a professional, you will always want to know what those differences are.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "What will you be expecting from such approaches?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You are going to be facing various levels of difficulty regarding program writing for our class.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Might Want to for What Is Advisable", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<strong>Modal verbs</strong> are used to express possibility, obligation, advisability, request, necessity, and ability. Today, we will focus on <strong>might want to</strong> for advisability. As you can see, the modal verb <strong>might</strong> is part of a longer structure that includes the verb want with its usual preposition <strong>to</strong>. Let's see how this structure works!   ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<strong>Grammar Structure</strong><br/> To use <strong>might want to</strong> in a sentence, follow this structure:<br/><br/>  Subject + might want to + verb + complement<br/><br/>  Example:<br/> <span class =\"italic\">I think they <strong>might want to continue</strong> the contract only if we complete the project on time.<br/><br/>   The case of <strong>might want to</strong> is very interesting because it permits to give a stronger emphasis to what you want to advise on, but with a high degree of politeness.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class =\"italic\">Your team <strong>might want to focus</strong> on program design, since they need to finish that first.<br/>You <strong>might want to study</strong> all the possible scenarios, as it is important to foresee any flaws in the system.</span>  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "It doesn't matter if you might want to focus on object-oriented programming, as it is the most popular paradigm nowadays.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If you are good at programming with POP, you might want to do the same with a more complex program.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "procedural ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "following a procedure ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: For developing a fairly complex program you will choose a procedural approach (POP).", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob02aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "step by step ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "in a way that follows a series of steps to complete a process", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: There's a set of instructions to be followed step by step. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob02aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "face", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to deal with ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: You are going to be facing various levels of difficulty regarding program writing for our class.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob02aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "modifiable ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "able to be changed or altered", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Well, they should make the language for writing a program easier, bug-free, less hard to understand, easily modifiable, etc.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob02aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "bug free  ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "with no code errors ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A programming approach should make the process of writing a program easier, easily modifiable, and bug-free.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob02aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "paradigm ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a model or a set of perspectives to understand and define a topic or reality", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": " Example: Paradigms in programming are going to remain essential for a very long time.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob02aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Look for further information about POP and OOP write down notes. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Create a chart in which you show the main features of each one in specific situations, provide examples.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share with your partners.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I look for information about POP and OOP ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I create a chart to show the main features of each paradigm. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about programming paradigms.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": " I 